      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "1. Since the lines intersect at the point $(6, 10)$, we can substitute $x = 6$ and $y = 10$ into the equations of the lines to get a system of equations:\n\nAllowing $x$ and $y$ to be generalized as $x = x$ and $y = y$ gives us the following system:\n   \\alignat{2}\n   10 &= mx + 4    &&\\text{(Equation 1)} \\\\\n   10 &= 3x + b    &&\\text{(Equation 2)}\n   \\endalignat{2}\n\n2. Next, we solve for $x$ and $y$ by substituting the given point into the equations:\n\nSubstituting $x = 6$ and $y = 10$ into Equation 1, we get:\n   \\[\n   10 = m(6) + 4\n   \\]\n\nSimplifying this equation gives us:\n   \\[\n   10 = 6m + 4 \\implies 6 = 6m \\implies m = 1\n   \\]\n\n3. Now that we have $m = 1$, we substitute it into Equation 2 to find the value of $b$:\n\nSubstituting $m = 1$ into Equation 2, we get:\n   \\[\n   10 = 3(6) + b\n   \\]\n\nSimplifying this equation gives us:\n   \\[\n   10 = 18 + b \\implies b = 10 - 18 \\implies b = -8\n   \\]\n\n4. Finally, to find the value of $b + m$, we add the values of $b$ and $m$:\n\n\\[\nb + m = -8 + 1 = \\boxed{-7}\n\\]",
