      "text": "To determine the value of \\( b + m \\), we start by using the fact that the two lines intersect at the point \\((6, 10)\\). This means that at \\( x = 6 \\), both equations yield \\( y = 10 \\).\n\nFirst, substitute \\( x = 6 \\) and \\( y = 10 \\) into the equation of the first line \\( y = mx + 4 \\):\n\\[\n10 = m \\cdot 6 + 4\n\\]\nSolving for \\( m \\), we get:\n\\[\n10 = 6m + 4 \\implies 10 - 4 = 6m \\implies 6 = 6m \\implies m = 1\n\\]\n\nNext, substitute \\( x = 6 \\) and \\( y = 10 \\) into the equation of the second line \\( y = 3x + b \\):\n\\[\n10 = 3 \\cdot 6 + b\n\\]\nSolving for \\( b \\), we get:\n\\[\n10 = 18 + b \\implies 10 - 18 = b \\implies b = -8\n\\]\n\nNow, we need to find the value of \\( b + m \\):\n\\[\nb + m = -8 + 1 = -7\n\\]\n\nThus, the value of \\( b + m \\) is \\(\\boxed{-7}\\).",
